---
import { Picture } from 'astro:assets';
import SolidButton from '../primitives/SolidButton.astro';
import SectionIndex from '../primitives/SectionIndex.astro';
import PillButton from '../primitives/PillButton.astro';
import { getHeroBanners, getSiteSettings, heroClock } from '../../lib/site-content';
import { getCategories, getDivisions, getProducts } from '../../lib/catalog';

/**
 * Hero — headline, an empty banner band, and the two CTAs.
 *
 * THE BAND IS EMPTY ON PURPOSE, as of 2026-08-20. The client asked for the
 * mockup's insertable slot and for the existing posters to go. All six were
 * deleted — artwork and data entries — because they are portrait (1261:1561)
 * and the slot is specified at 2800 x 700, a 4:1 band. Letterboxing a portrait
 * poster into a 4:1 strip would have been worse than showing the shape
 * honestly and waiting for the right artwork.
 *
 * THE CAROUSEL CODE IS STILL HERE, and that is deliberate rather than
 * forgetful. Since 2026-08-23 an admin uploads a banner at /admin/banners and
 * switches it on, and it renders here — clock and pips and pause included —
 * at the next build. That is the seam doing its job.
 *
 * TWO BANNERS MUST NEVER COME BACK UNCORRECTED. The Grip Guard GP1 poster
 * prints an EN 388 icon reading 4X43D while the glove's own label —
 * photographed in that same banner — reads 4131X, overstating cut resistance
 * as D where the glove says X, NOT TESTED. The Orbit Fan poster labels the fan
 * FW-40W, a code belonging to no product (handoff.md §17). Both are in
 * BACKLOG.md for reissue.
 *
 * **NOTHING IN CODE STOPS EITHER ONE ANY MORE, AND THAT IS A REAL LOSS.**
 * site-content.test.ts used to assert that neither filename was enabled. An
 * uploaded banner has a generated path and an admin-chosen name, so no test can
 * recognise those two artworks. The protection is now this paragraph, the two
 * BACKLOG.md items, and a person not uploading them. If either is reissued,
 * correct the artwork FIRST.
 *
 * DECORATIVE EITHER WAY. Slides carry alt="" and the track is aria-hidden;
 * the empty slot is aria-hidden too. These are marketing posters whose content
 * is baked-in text — headlines, spec blocks, a QR code, a phone number — that
 * alt text cannot reproduce, and every product they show is reachable in the
 * catalogue below. The <h1> and the two CTAs carry the hero's meaning.
 *
 * IT AUTO-ADVANCES WHEN IT HAS SLIDES, SO IT NEEDS A PAUSE MECHANISM. WCAG
 * 2.2.2 applies to any motion that starts automatically and runs past five
 * seconds, decorative or not, and axe does not test for it. The control is a
 * checkbox read by `:has()`, deliberately not an island — a pause button that
 * needs JavaScript to exist is no use to a visitor whose JavaScript is off,
 * and the animation runs for them regardless. The whole control row is absent
 * in the empty state, because a pause button with nothing to pause is a
 * control that cannot do what it says.
 *
 * NO SCRIPT AT ALL NOW. The carousel is pure CSS and the cursor parallax was
 * deleted with the side-by-side layout — it moved the stage against the copy
 * beside it, and a centred column has nothing to move against. That took the
 * page's inline-script count from 9 to 8: `npm run csp` re-run and
 * vercel.json committed with it.
 */

/*
 * WHICH BANNERS, AND IN WHAT ORDER, IS DATA — see src/lib/site-content.ts.
 *
 * It was a literal array here until 2026-08-19. Moving it behind the seam is
 * what makes banner selection editable from /admin later without touching this
 * component, and it is the same structural decision rule 3 makes for products.
 *
 * The order still matters and is set in the data: alternating Safety and
 * Electricals keeps consecutive slides from sharing a palette — spill control
 * is red and dark, the highbay a blue-lit warehouse, solar a violet dusk, the
 * coveralls orange, the vests hi-viz yellow, the insect killer near-white.
 *
 * SINCE 2026-08-23 THE ARTWORK IS UPLOADED, NOT COMMITTED. The files live in a
 * private Supabase Storage bucket and `getHeroBanners()` hands back a
 * short-lived signed URL per banner. That URL is spent HERE, at build time:
 * `<Picture>` downloads it, re-encodes it and emits local assets, so the page a
 * visitor gets still references `/_astro/*` and `img-src 'self'` never widens.
 *
 * The `import.meta.glob` this replaced could only ever see committed files,
 * which is why an admin could not add a banner before. The rule it carried is
 * kept and moved: a banner that cannot be resolved throws in
 * `src/lib/site-content.ts` rather than rendering a gap in the carousel.
 *
 * THIS COMPONENT MUST NEVER IMPORT SUPABASE. `npm run verify` fails if the
 * service-role key's name appears anywhere under src/components, and it is
 * right to — Vite inlines `import.meta.env.*` at build time. It calls
 * `getHeroBanners()` and nothing else.
 */
const BANNERS = await getHeroBanners();

/*
 * The track carries one extra slide: a second copy of the first.
 *
 * The animation runs 0 -> -N00% and then restarts at 0. Without the repeat that
 * final frame would be blank, and a "rewind" back through every slide is the
 * alternative — visible, and wrong. Landing on a copy of slide 1 and cutting
 * back to the real slide 1 is invisible because the two are the same image.
 * Ticker.astro duplicates its track for the same reason.
 */
const SLIDES = BANNERS.length > 0 ? [...BANNERS, BANNERS[0]] : [];

/*
 * THE CLOCK, AND WHY IT IS COMPUTED RATHER THAN TYPED.
 *
 * The track's keyframe stops, the pips' stagger and the cycle length are ONE
 * system derived from the slide count. While the count was a literal six, so
 * were all three — and enabling a seventh banner would then have produced a
 * carousel whose lit pip reported a slide that was not showing. That reads as a
 * rendering bug and is arithmetic, which is the worst kind to debug.
 *
 * So the count is the only input. Each slide holds for HOLD seconds and moves
 * for SLIDE seconds; the cycle is the sum times the count. At six banners this
 * computes to exactly the values that were previously hard-coded — 42s, stops
 * at 14.286% and 16.667%, pips every 7s — which is what makes this refactor
 * verifiable: the rendered page does not change.
 *
 * ASTRO SCOPED STYLES CANNOT INTERPOLATE A FRONTMATTER VALUE. That is why the
 * generated rules go in an `is:inline` <style> below rather than in the scoped
 * block, and it is the same constraint that forces `Hero.astro`'s PORTRAIT
 * media query to be written twice in the retained-artwork version. It costs no
 * CSP hash: `style-src` is `'self' 'unsafe-inline'`, unlike `script-src`, which
 * is hash-based with no 'unsafe-inline' at all.
 */
/*
 * NULL WHEN THERE ARE NO BANNERS, and the guard is load-bearing rather than
 * defensive. `heroClock` throws below one slide, on purpose — a carousel with
 * nothing in it is a bug everywhere except here, where an empty stage is the
 * shipped design. So the empty case is handled at the one place that knows it
 * is legitimate, and the throw stays intact for every other caller.
 */
const clock = BANNERS.length > 0 ? heroClock(BANNERS.length) : null;

const pct = (n: number) => `${Number(n.toFixed(3))}%`;
/** `translateX(0)` for the first slide rather than `-0%`, which computes the same and reads as a typo. */
const shift = (i: number) => (i === 0 ? '0' : `-${i * 100}%`);

const carouselCss = clock === null ? '' : `
@keyframes hero-carousel {
${BANNERS.map(
  (_, i) =>
    `  ${pct(i * clock!.stepPct)}, ${pct(i * clock!.stepPct + clock!.holdPct)} { transform: translateX(${shift(i)}); }`,
).join('\n')}
  100% { transform: translateX(${shift(BANNERS.length)}); }
}
@keyframes hero-pip {
  0% { background: var(--accent-fill); flex-grow: 2.6; }
  ${pct(clock!.stepPct)}, 100% { background: var(--line-control); flex-grow: 1; }
}
[data-hero-stage] .hero__track,
[data-hero-stage] .hero__pip { animation-duration: ${clock!.cycleSeconds}s; }
${clock!.pipDelays
  .map((delay, i) => `[data-hero-stage] .hero__pip:nth-child(${i + 1}) { animation-delay: ${delay}s; }`)
  .join('\n')}
`.trim();

/*
 * THE BYTE BUDGET, WHICH IS THE REAL COST OF THIS CHANGE.
 *
 * The helmet was one 18 KB AVIF. A full-width banner is not, and pretending
 * otherwise would be the kind of unmeasured claim handoff.md §12 exists to warn
 * about — a 23 KB font cost this page a Lighthouse point once.
 *
 * THESE NUMBERS WERE RE-DERIVED ON 2026-08-23 AND THE OLD ONES WERE WRONG.
 * `widths` stopped at 600 and `sizes` claimed 436px, both correct for the
 * portrait card this hero used to carry beside its copy. The redesigned hero
 * puts the band across the full column, and measured in a browser it renders
 * 1176px wide on a desktop and 350px on a 390px phone. The old numbers handed a
 * 436px source to a 1174px box — a 2.7x upscale, on the largest image on the
 * site, and nothing failed because an upscaled image is still an image.
 *
 * So `sizes` states the measured layout: capped at 1176px once the column stops
 * growing at 1216px, and `calc(100vw - 40px)` below that, which is the 40px of
 * gutter measured at 390px. `widths` runs to 2352 — 1176 at DPR 2 — and Astro
 * clamps to the source, so an upload at the 1400px floor simply gets fewer
 * variants rather than an upscaled one.
 *
 * `quality={42}` is kept. Astro default sits ABOVE it — asking for 58 made the
 * files BIGGER, which is worth knowing before someone "optimises" by raising
 * it. It was checked at 2x against the old artwork's QR code and re-checked
 * against a 2000x486 banner at DPR 2 on 2026-08-23.
 */
const WIDTHS = [390, 700, 1176, 1600, 2352];
const SIZES = '(min-width: 1216px) 1176px, calc(100vw - 40px)';
/*
 * THE HERO COUNTS THE CATALOGUE, AND IT GOES THROUGH THE SEAM.
 *
 * Rule 3: never `src/data/*`, never `getCollection`. This is the same read
 * `src/pages/index.astro` already performs for its meta description, and for
 * the same reason — the only number on that page that could go stale was a
 * typed one. Both this component and that page are prerendered, so the read
 * happens at build time and costs a visitor nothing.
 *
 * RULE 1 IS WHY THE SENTENCE SAYS WHAT IT SAYS. The design review this came
 * from proposed "1,000+ PRODUCTS" and "QUALITY ASSURED" as trust markers. The
 * catalogue holds 94 products, and there is no source on this machine for a
 * certification claim. A counted number cannot drift into a fabricated one.
 */
const [divisions, categories, products] = await Promise.all([
  getDivisions(),
  getCategories(),
  getProducts(),
]);

/* "Spartan Electricals · Spartan Safety" says the brand twice under a logo
   that has just said it. The ranges are what the eyebrow is for. */
const ranges = divisions.map((d) => d.name.replace(/^Spartan\s+/, '')).join(' + ');

/* The founding year, back as metadata rather than as a badge. It left the hero
   on 2026-08-29 because a hexagonal "Est. 2015" was occupying the most valuable
   line on the page for a fact that is not a proposition. As one segment of a
   technical masthead it costs nothing and adds provenance. */
const settings = await getSiteSettings();

---

<section class="hero">
  <div class="hero__grid" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  {/* THE CROP MARKS AND THE TWO EDGE TICKS ARE GONE — 2026-08-29.

      Four registration marks at the hero's corners and a red tick at each
      edge. They were the first attempt at a technical register and they were
      the wrong one: they repeated nowhere else on the page, carried no
      hierarchy, sat far enough out to read as detached from the content, and
      cost a visual layer for no payoff. A review put it plainly — delete them.

      The register they were reaching for now exists and is systematic: the dot
      grid, the section numerals, the mono micro-labels, the red vertical rule
      and the thin heads. Adding crop marks on top of that is decoration about
      decoration.

      `tests/e2e/hero-marks.spec.ts` WENT WITH THEM, and that deserves saying
      out loud. The client raised a real defect against their own mockup — the
      bottom marks landing on the CTAs — and that spec was written so deleting
      the marks could not read as a passing fix. Deleting it alongside the
      decoration is not that dodge: a collision between a mark and a button
      cannot recur once no mark exists. If any of this comes back, the spec
      comes back with it. */}


  {/* ONE CENTRED COLUMN, AND THE SOURCE-ORDER SPLIT IS GONE WITH IT.

      This used to be two `.wrap`s with the stage between them, because the
      desktop layout floated the stage beside the copy while the phone stacked
      badge, headline, stage, CTAs — and CSS `order` could not interleave a
      child of `.hero` with a grandchild of `.hero__wrap`. Splitting the
      markup was the only way to express both.

      The client asked for the mockup's centred stack on 2026-08-20, so both
      layouts are now the same order and the split has nothing left to buy. One
      column, one wrap, DOM order is the layout at every width. */}
  <div class="wrap hero__wrap">
    {/* THE MASTHEAD — a technical system line, replacing the hexagonal badge
        and the two red rules that used to flank it.

        Three segments, and every one is read from somewhere rather than typed:
        the brand, the divisions through the catalogue seam, the founding year
        through site-content. It reads like the header of a specification
        sheet, which is what this company's own product pages already look
        like — the layout coming out of the brand rather than industrial
        styling applied on top of it.

        The slashes are aria-hidden. A screen reader announcing "slash" three
        times is noise; the segments read perfectly well as a list. */}
    <p class="hero__masthead">
      {/* EVERY SEGMENT IS ITS OWN SPAN, and that is required rather than tidy.
          A bare text node inside a flex container becomes an anonymous flex
          item, so "Spartan" and the registered mark were two items with the
          row's 10px gap between them — it rendered as "SPARTAN ®". Wrapping
          each segment makes the gap fall between segments, where it belongs. */}
      <span class="hero__masthead-item">Spartan<span class="hero__masthead-reg">&#174;</span></span>
      <span class="hero__masthead-sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="hero__masthead-item">{ranges}</span>
    </p>

    {/* THE COMPOSITION: headline left, counted data right.

        Everything here used to be centred — eyebrow, headline, rule, sentence,
        band — and a design review on 2026-08-29 was blunt about the cost:
        "that much symmetry is killing the energy". It was right. A brand whose
        whole identity is angular and industrial was laying itself out like a
        restrained corporate brochure.

        What replaces it is a deliberate imbalance. The headline runs from the
        left margin and its second line is pushed right and set larger, so the
        two lines lean against each other instead of stacking politely. The
        counted totals become the counterweight on the right — the review's own
        best idea, that "94 products in 15 categories" is design material and
        should be used as such rather than buried inside a sentence. */}
    {/* THE COMPOSITION — headline left, the section numeral as the in-flow
        counterweight, and the counted totals as a spec strip underneath.

        THIS IS THE FOURTH SHAPE THIS BLOCK HAS TAKEN and the first one built
        on real edges. A client screenshot of the previous version showed what
        every earlier shape shared: elements aligned to numbers instead of to
        each other. The ghost numeral floated absolutely in the top-right and
        read as a smudge behind the stats; the stats rail bottom-hung in space;
        the banner stopped at 93.5% so its right edge met nothing.

        Now there are exactly two vertical edges — the wrap's left and right —
        and everything lands on one of them: masthead, headline, lede, spec
        strip and CTAs on the left; numeral, carousel controls and the banner's
        right edge on the right. The numeral is a grid cell, not a coordinate,
        so it cannot smudge, clip or drift; it is the same in-flow treatment
        every section head uses, which finally makes the hero the system's
        first instance instead of its exception. */}
    <div class="hero__composition">
      <div class="hero__type">
        {/* ONE LINE IN THE SOURCE, ON PURPOSE. `tests/e2e/home.spec.ts` reads
            `textContent` directly rather than through a matcher that collapses
            whitespace, because "Home andindustrial" has shipped once already.
            A newline between these two spans puts it straight back, and the
            trailing space belongs INSIDE the first span for the same reason. */}
        <h1 class="hero__title"><span class="hero__title-a">Home and industrial </span><span class="hero__title-b">solutions.</span></h1>

        <p class="hero__lede">
          Lighting, ventilation, water management, PPE and workwear for
          contractors and distributors.
        </p>
      </div>

      <SectionIndex n="01" flow />
    </div>

    <div class="hero__stage" data-hero-stage>
      {
        BANNERS.length === 0 ? (
          /* THE EMPTY SLOT — the shipped state as of 2026-08-20.

             The six posters were deleted on instruction: they are portrait
             (1261:1561) and cannot fill a 4:1 band, so letterboxing them into
             one would have been worse than showing the shape honestly.

             aria-hidden, because it is a note to whoever supplies the artwork
             and not content. A screen-reader user has no use for "2800 x 700"
             and the <h1> and CTAs carry the hero's actual meaning, exactly as
             they did when this held a carousel.

             THE MOCKUP'S "or browse files" LINK IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE. It
             would be an affordance that does nothing — the same defect this
             repo has already removed twice, from the footer's newsletter field
             and its three href="#" social icons, both recorded in Footer.astro.
             Real upload is Stage 6 of the admin content plan and belongs there,
             not faked here. */
          <div class="hero__slot" aria-hidden="true">
            <svg class="hero__slot-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
              <rect x="3" y="4" width="18" height="16" rx="2" />
              <circle cx="8.5" cy="9.5" r="1.6" />
              <path d="m3.5 17 4.8-4.4a1.8 1.8 0 0 1 2.4 0L16 17" />
              <path d="m14 14.4 1.7-1.5a1.8 1.8 0 0 1 2.4 0l2.4 2.1" />
            </svg>
            {/* The dimensions are one token and must not break across lines —
                "2800 ×" on one line and "700 (4:1)" on the next reads as two
                numbers rather than one size. */}
            <p class="hero__slot-label">
              Drop the horizontal hero banner —{' '}
              <span class="hero__slot-dim">2800 &times; 700 (4:1)</span>
            </p>
          </div>
        ) : (
          <>
            {/* The checkbox is the state and the label is the control, so the
                pause works with no JavaScript at all. `:has()` on the stage
                reads it.

                The accessible name has to START with the visible label text
                ("Pause"), or the mismatch is itself a 2.5.3 Label in Name
                failure — the same defect that sat on every product card at a
                green axe score. */}
            <input
              class="hero__toggle"
              type="checkbox"
              id="hero-carousel-pause"
              role="switch"
              aria-label="Pause the rotating product banners"
            />

            {/* THE CAMPAIGN BAR — the module's label and its controls on one
                row, above the band.

                They were two separate horizontal strips: a "FEATURED" label
                with a rule trailing off to the right, then the artwork, then a
                progress rail spanning the band's full width. A design review
                on 2026-08-29 counted the rules in this hero and asked for half
                of them gone, and it was right that a progress indicator has no
                reason to mirror a 1160px band. Merging the two strips removes
                one, and the trailing rule went with it.

                ABOVE THE BAND, NOT OVER IT. The constraint that put these
                below the artwork was that the banners carry near-white footer
                strips, so a white pip on one is invisible and a scrim dark
                enough to fix that covers the client's own QR code. Above sits
                on the page's own surface and satisfies it just as well.

                NO FAKE INDEX. "01 / FEATURED" was the review's suggestion and
                the 01 would have meant nothing — there is no section 02. The
                counter to its right is a real one. */}
            <div class="hero__bar">
              <p class="hero__frame-label">
                Featured<span class="hero__bar-sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>campaign
              </p>

              {/* THE WHOLE ROW IS ABSENT IN THE EMPTY STATE. A pause button with
                  nothing to pause is the same dead affordance as the "browse
                  files" link this component refuses, and WCAG 2.2.2 only asks
                  for a control when something actually moves. */}
            <div class="hero__controls">
              {/* ONE ROW, ONE SYSTEM, since 2026-08-29. It was pips hard left
                  and a Pause label hard right with `space-between` throwing the
                  gap between them, which read as two unrelated widgets rather
                  than as one control. Now: a counter, the pips as a continuous
                  track, and the pause — left to right, like an instrument.

                  STILL BELOW THE BAND, NEVER OVER IT. The banners carry
                  near-white footer strips; a white pip on one is invisible, and
                  a scrim dark enough to fix that covers the client's own QR
                  code. That was found by looking at the rendered page.

                  THE COUNTER IS aria-hidden WITH THE PIPS. The track is
                  decorative and every product in it is a real link further down
                  the page, so announcing "01 of 03" would narrate a carousel a
                  screen reader has already been told to ignore. The total is
                  derived, never typed — a fourth banner must not need an edit
                  here. */}
              <p class="hero__count" aria-hidden="true">
                <span class="hero__count-now">01</span>
                <span class="hero__count-sep">/</span>
                <span class="hero__count-total">{String(BANNERS.length).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
              </p>

              <div class="hero__pips" aria-hidden="true">
                {BANNERS.map(() => (
                  <span class="hero__pip" />
                ))}
              </div>

              <label class="hero__pause" for="hero-carousel-pause">
                Pause
              </label>
            </div>
            </div>

            <figure class="hero__frame">
              <div class="hero__track" aria-hidden="true">
                {SLIDES.map((banner, i) => (
                  <div class="hero__slide">
                                        <Picture
                      src={banner.src}
                      alt=""
                      widths={WIDTHS}
                      sizes={SIZES}
                      formats={['avif', 'webp']}
                      fallbackFormat="jpg"
                      quality={42}
                      loading={i === 0 ? 'eager' : 'lazy'}
                      fetchpriority={i === 0 ? 'high' : 'auto'}
                      decoding="async"
                    />
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>
            </figure>

          </>
        )
      }
    </div>

    {/* THE CLOSING ROW — actions left, proof right, one full-measure line.

        The spec strip lived directly under the lede for one revision and cost
        the fold dearly: it pushed the banner and both CTAs ~78px down, putting
        BROWSE CATALOGUE below the crease of an 850px laptop — while the CTA
        row's right half sat empty. Pairing them solves both at once, and it is
        the stronger composition anyway: the visitor is asked to act at the
        exact moment the counted totals argue they should, and the row spans
        wrap edge to wrap edge like everything above it.

        The dl is unchanged — same counted values, same tests. Geometry only. */}
    <div class="hero__actions">
      <SolidButton href="/catalogue">Browse catalogue</SolidButton>
      <PillButton href="/enquiry">Request a quote</PillButton>

      <dl class="hero__index">
        <div class="hero__index-row">
          <dt>Products</dt>
          <dd>{String(products.length).padStart(2, '0')}</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="hero__index-row">
          <dt>Categories</dt>
          <dd>{String(categories.length).padStart(2, '0')}</dd>
        </div>
        {/* WAS "DIVISIONS / 02" — a review called it a weak statistic that made
            the range sound unintentionally small, and the divisions are already
            named in the masthead. The founding year is the one credibility
            figure on file; the suggested replacements (dealers, markets,
            territories) are all unsourceable here and refused under rule 1. */}
        <div class="hero__index-row">
          <dt>Established</dt>
          <dd>{settings.established}</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /*
   * THE CLOCK. Six slides, seven seconds each. Every animation below is 42s and
   * differs only by delay, which is what keeps the pips locked to the track
   * without any script. Changing the slide count means changing this, the
   * keyframe percentages, and the pip delay — they are one system.
   */
  /* 760px, up from the helmet hero's 660px. The helmet was a transparent PNG
     whose dome stopped well short of its own canvas edge, so a 660px stage could
     overlap the absolutely positioned header and nothing showed. A banner is an
     opaque rectangle with a hard top edge: at 660px it ran from y=0 to y=660 and
     the nav rendered directly on top of the artwork — measured, "Industries" and
     "Contact" sat over the poster's own headline. The card now lives entirely in
     the band below the header, and the hero is as tall as that band needs to be
     for a 4:5 portrait to have any presence beside a 76px headline. */
  /* THE CENTRED STACK — the client's mockup, adopted 2026-08-20.

     Everything here used to be geometry for a stage that floated to the right
     of the copy: a 760px min-height so a 4:5 portrait had presence beside a
     76px headline, an absolutely positioned stage with a negative top margin,
     and a two-row grid with align-content to stop the CTAs drifting to the
     bottom edge. None of it survives a single centred column, and keeping any
     of it would have been geometry defending a layout that no longer exists.

     The height is now whatever the content needs. padding-top clears the
     absolutely positioned chrome above it — an 84px nav plus its 2px progress
     rule is 86px, and the extra is the mockup's breathing room above the
     eyebrow. */
  .hero {
    position: relative;
    background: var(--surface-alt);
    overflow: hidden;
    /* 86px of chrome plus 22px of breathing room above the eyebrow — the same
       22px the old 152/130 pair carried before the utility bar was removed. */
    padding: 108px 0 76px;
    /* WAS `center`. Every child inherited it and the result was a column of
       centred strips — eyebrow, headline, rule, sentence, band — which a design
       review on 2026-08-29 named as the single biggest source of blandness on
       the page. Left is the axis now, and the imbalance is built on top of it
       deliberately rather than avoided. */
    text-align: left;

    /* WHERE THE HEADER CHROME ENDS, and every decoration below starts there.

       The hero runs from y=0 because the nav is absolutely positioned over it,
       so without this the dot field and the crop marks run up behind the logo
       and the nav links. Measured rather than assumed: the nav occupies 0 to
       84 and its progress rule 84 to 86.

       It was 130 while a 44px utility bar sat above the nav. That bar is gone
       (2026-08-29) and this moved with it.

       THESE THREE NUMBERS ARE ONE ARITHMETIC CHAIN — this constant, `.hero`'s
       padding-top, and the padding-top in the `max-width: 1180px` block below.
       Change the header's height without changing all three and the dot field
       paints behind the nav again, with every gate still green. */
    --hero-chrome: 86px;

    /* THE HERO'S VERTICAL SCALE, added 2026-08-29.

       Five hand-tuned margins (22 / 26 / 30 / 14 / 28) became four names on a
       4px grid, so a relationship can be reasoned about rather than nudged.
       Read top to bottom: gaps tighten as the eye moves down inside a group
       and open between groups. Anything that changes one of these should be
       able to say which group boundary it is moving. */
    --hero-gap-tight: 12px;   /* inside a group: rule to lede, band to controls */
    --hero-gap-close: 20px;   /* headline to its own rule */
    --hero-gap-step: 32px;    /* group to group: eyebrow to headline, lede to stage */
    --hero-gap-open: 40px;    /* the stage group to the CTA row */
  }

  /* The 80px graph paper behind everything. Decorative and very low contrast,
     so it never competes with the copy. */
  /* A 22px dot field, replacing the 80px white line grid the dark hero used.
     It reads as a drawing sheet rather than graph paper, which is what the
     client's mockup asked for, and it survives being scaled to a phone in a way
     an 80px grid does not. */
  /* STRONGER DOTS, BUT MASKED — the two go together and neither works alone.

     At --line (#e4e4e7, 1.27:1 against the band) the field was barely there on
     a bright screen. Simply darkening it puts texture directly behind a 76px
     headline and a dashed slot, and the page starts to read as busy rather
     than considered.

     So the dots are darker AND denser at the edges, fading out through the
     middle where the content sits. The mask does the work: the type and the
     slot land on clean ground, the frame keeps its drawing-sheet texture, and
     the grid reads as a deliberate border treatment rather than wallpaper.

     `--hero-dot` is a local token, not a semantic one. It is decoration with
     no contrast requirement, so it has no place in the global scale, and
     naming it here keeps the two dot rules below in step. */
  .hero__grid {
    position: absolute;
    /* Starts below the header, not at the top of the hero. The nav sits on
       flat --surface-alt now instead of on texture. */
    inset: var(--hero-chrome) 0 0 0;
    --hero-dot: #c2c2ca;
    background-image: radial-gradient(var(--hero-dot) 1.4px, transparent 1.4px);
    background-size: 22px 22px;
    /* The mask never reaches full transparency. A first attempt used
       `transparent 38%` and cleared the dots across most of the visible band,
       which made the field LESS present than the faint version it replaced.
       Holding the centre at 30% alpha keeps the texture continuous across the
       whole hero while still easing it behind the copy.

       WIDENED AND LOWERED 2026-08-29, alpha untouched. The eased zone was
       tuned when the column was eyebrow, headline, rule, banner; a supporting
       sentence now sits between the rule and the banner and pushes the band
       down. 62%/58% at 52% is the ellipse that still covers the copy AND the
       artwork, which is what the design review asked for — the dots were
       running at full strength directly behind the banner and competing with
       it. The 30% floor is deliberately NOT reduced: that is the change the
       comment above records as having already failed once. */
    -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(
      ellipse 62% 58% at 50% 52%,
      rgb(0 0 0 / 30%) 0%,
      #000 72%
    );
    mask-image: radial-gradient(ellipse 62% 58% at 50% 52%, rgb(0 0 0 / 30%) 0%, #000 72%);
  }

  .hero__stage {
    /* WRAP EDGE TO WRAP EDGE — the >=901px cap at 93.5% is gone. It made the
       band stop short of the measure "to give ~6% back", which left its right
       edge meeting nothing: an orphan sliver of dot grid between the artwork
       and the margin, in a client screenshot, aligned with nothing. The hero
       has exactly two vertical edges now and the band lands on both. Its
       dominance is governed by what it always really was: the fixed 4:1 ratio
       and the frame. */
    /* FULL COLUMN WIDTH HERE, AND THE ~6% GIVEN BACK ONLY ON A WIDE SCREEN.

       The band used to cap at 1160px against a 1240px wrap — the ~6% it gave
       back so uploaded campaign artwork could not set the visual temperature
       of the page. When the wrap widened to 1360 that fixed number would have
       silently handed the 6% back, so it became the ratio it always was.

       A RATIO BINDS EVERYWHERE, WHICH A FIXED WIDTH DID NOT, and that was the
       bug: 93.5% of a 320px phone column is 299px, so the band stopped
       spanning the column on a phone. `tests/e2e/hero-mobile.spec.ts` measures
       exactly that and caught it. On a phone there is no surplus width to give
       back and nothing to protect the page from — the band IS the column. */
    max-width: 100%;
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    margin-top: var(--hero-gap-step);
    z-index: 1;
  }

  /* THE EMPTY SLOT.

     4:1 wherever there is width to hold it. Below 720px a 4:1 band is under
     90px tall on a phone — too short to read as a banner and too short to hold
     its own label — so it opens out to 3:2, which is the proportion the mobile
     mockup draws. That is a real tension worth stating rather than hiding: a
     single 2800 x 700 artwork cannot fill both — which is why only the EMPTY
     slot opens out. See the media query below: `.hero__frame` holds 4:1 at
     every width so a real banner is never cropped. */
  .hero__slot {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 12px;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 1;
    border: 1px dashed var(--line-control);
    background: var(--surface-page);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    padding: 24px;
  }

  .hero__slot-icon {
    width: 26px;
    height: 26px;
  }

  .hero__slot-label {
    font-size: var(--fs-ui);
    line-height: 1.5;
    max-width: 44ch;
  }

  .hero__slot-dim {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* Carried over from the helmet stage so the card is not a bright rectangle
     sitting on flat black with nothing between them. Sized to the card rather
     than to the old 540px circle, and softer — the artwork supplies its own
     colour now, so this only has to lift the card off the background. */
  /* Centred on the CARD rather than on the stage, because the stage now carries
     a control row underneath and centring on the whole thing would push the halo
     low. `bottom: 44px` is that row's height plus its gap. */

  /* The card. `aspect-ratio` rather than a height, so the box is the slot's
     shape and the control row can simply follow it in normal flow.

     4 / 1, AND IT MUST MATCH `.hero__slot` ABOVE. It was `1261 / 1561` until
     2026-08-23 — the native ratio of the six portrait posters, correct when
     they were the artwork and left behind when the hero was redesigned around a
     4:1 band. The empty slot was rebuilt to 4:1 and this was not, and because
     no banner existed to render, nothing showed it: the first real upload came
     back cropped to a tall centre slice of itself.

     The two ratios are one decision. If the slot's shape ever changes, change
     it here in the same commit. */
  /* Sits on the frame's top-left corner, in the technical register the crop
     marks and the slide counter already speak. The rule running off to the
     right is what makes it read as a drawing label rather than as a heading. */
  /* THE BAR — label left, controls right, one row above the band.

     `align-items: end` so the label's baseline and the counter's sit on the
     same line despite different sizes. */
  .hero__bar {
    display: flex;
    /* `wrap` is load-bearing, not defensive. The label and the control rail
       total ~380px at their floors; a 360px phone rendered the PAUSE button
       half off the edge, and the hero's own `overflow: hidden` swallowed the
       evidence — no scrollbar, just a clipped control. Wrapped, the rail drops
       to its own line and stays whole. */
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: end;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 10px 24px;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }

  /* THE TRAILING RULE IS GONE. This label used to end in a hairline running
     off to the right edge of the band — one of four horizontal rules a design
     review counted in a hero about 600px tall. The bar's own layout separates
     the label from the controls; a line to do the same job was decoration
     wearing a structural costume. */
  /* THE LABEL KEEPS ITS WORDS AND GAINS NO NUMBER.

     The third review suggested "01 / FEATURED CAMPAIGN". It would have been
     the third "01" above the fold — the section index behind the headline, the
     slide counter to the right of this very label, and then this. That is the
     same redundancy the review had just asked us to remove from the giant
     numeral, arriving from a different direction. The slide counter is the
     number this module needs and it already has it. */
  .hero__frame-label {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-label);
    font-weight: var(--fw-label);
    letter-spacing: 0.2em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  /* Matches the masthead's separator exactly, which is what ties the two
     technical lines together as one system rather than two ideas. */
  .hero__bar-sep {
    margin: 0 8px;
    color: var(--accent-text);
  }


  .hero__frame {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 1;
    margin: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    /* 2px, down from 4px on 2026-08-29, and not one more. The rectangle is
       this design's whole argument; the radius exists only to stop the corner
       reading as a clipping error. Rounded SaaS cards would make it worse. */
    border-radius: 2px;
    background: var(--surface-raised);
    /* Re-tuned for a white page. The dark hero carried 0.75-alpha black plus a
       red bloom; both were reading the black behind them. On white that shadow
       is grey mud and the bloom is the glow this theme deleted, so the frame
       now lifts with a single soft shadow and nothing else. */
    box-shadow: 0 24px 48px rgba(8, 8, 10, 0.14);
  }

  /*
   * The sliding track.
   *
   * `display: flex` with each slide at `flex: 0 0 100%` makes the track exactly
   * as wide as the frame while its seven children overflow it, so one
   * `translateX(-100%)` is exactly one slide. Setting an explicit 700% width
   * instead would make the percentages resolve against the track and every
   * number here would need dividing by seven.
   */
  .hero__track {
    display: flex;
    height: 100%;
    /* Duration is generated from the slide count; see the inline style block. */
    animation: hero-carousel var(--ease-out, cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1)) infinite;
    animation-duration: 42s;
  }

  .hero__slide {
    flex: 0 0 100%;
    height: 100%;
  }

  /* `<Picture>` wraps the img in a real <picture>, which is inline by default —
     an inline box would sit on the text baseline and leave a strip of card
     showing under every slide. Both the wrapper and the img have to be told to
     fill. `:global` because Astro's scoping cannot reach inside a child
     component's rendered markup. */
  .hero__slide :global(picture) {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
  }

  .hero__slide :global(img) {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    display: block;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  /* The control row: pips left, pause right, on the hero's own black. */
  /* A rail, not three scattered parts. `space-between` is gone deliberately:
     it was what pushed the pips and the pause to opposite edges of a 940px
     band. The pips take the free space instead, so the row reads as one
     instrument at any width.

     NO max-width HERE. It was written with one at 940px — the measure the
     crest and the closing rule use — and that is the copy column, not the
     band: measured at 1440, the rail came out 940 wide against a 1176 frame
     and sat visibly inset from the artwork it controls. The stage is the
     parent, so inheriting its width is what keeps the two edges aligned at
     every viewport, and no number has to be kept in sync with anything. */
  /* COMPRESSED TO ~340px, down from the band's full width.

     The rail used to span all 1160px of the frame, and a design review put it
     plainly: there is no reason a progress indicator has to mirror the width
     of the thing it indexes. At full width the three segments read as another
     horizontal rule across the page — the fourth in a hero that had too many.
     At 340 it reads as an instrument.

     It sits in `.hero__bar` now, so it has no margin of its own; the bar
     places it. */
  .hero__controls {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    gap: 10px;
    /* 340 -> 300 at the ceiling, and the gap with it: a third review still
       found the assembly loose. Roughly 12% out of the whole unit. */
    width: clamp(185px, 22vw, 300px);
    height: 24px;
  }

  /* Data, not language — a slide index is exactly what --font-mono is for, and
     tabular figures stop the row twitching as the number changes. */
  .hero__count {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 4px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-label);
    font-weight: var(--fw-label);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  /* --accent-text, NOT --accent. 11px is far below the 24px large-text bar and
     brand red is 4.30:1 on this surface; --accent-text is 9.07:1. Rule 4. */
  .hero__count-now {
    color: var(--accent-text);
  }

  .hero__count-sep {
    opacity: 0.5;
  }

  /* The pips fill the rail rather than clustering at one end, so the row reads
     as a progress track rather than as three dots beside a button. */
  .hero__pips {
    display: flex;
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 4px;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* Non-text UI, which WCAG 1.4.11 holds to 3:1 against what is adjacent rather
     than to 4.5:1. This is only decidable because the row sits on a known
     surface — over the artwork, where these started, no ratio could be stated at
     all.

     The dark-theme pair was #5c5c66 and #ffffff on --color-black, and the
     history is worth keeping: this shipped for an hour at #3a3a42, a value
     picked to look right and asserted at "3.2:1" from memory when it actually
     measured 1.79:1. That is exactly what rule 4 means by "colour is measured,
     not chosen", and it got past a green axe run — axe does not evaluate 1.4.11
     on decorative-looking custom elements.

     Re-measured for the white theme against --surface-alt #f6f6f7, not carried
     over and not estimated:

       inactive --line-control #8a8a92 on #f6f6f7   3.17:1   passes the 3:1 bar
       active   --accent       #eb2927 on #f6f6f7   3.99:1   passes the 3:1 bar

     The active pip is brand red rather than ink: it is non-text UI at 3.99:1,
     well clear of its bar, and red is what the client's mockup shows. */
  /* GEOMETRY CHANGED 2026-08-29, TIMING DID NOT. `width: 18px` became a flex
     segment so the pips span the rail; the animation and its duration are
     carried across untouched. They are the clock — the slide count, the
     keyframe percentages and the per-pip delays are one system, described at
     the top of this style block and generated at the foot of this file.

     THE KEYFRAME HAD TO CHANGE WITH THE GEOMETRY, and for a while it did not.
     It animated `width` between 26px and 18px, which a flex item with
     `flex-basis: 0` ignores completely — so for one revision the only thing
     distinguishing the active segment was its colour, and a third review said
     the progress state read as too subtle. It was. The keyframe animates
     `flex-grow` now, so the current segment is visibly wider AND red, which is
     what a progress rail is supposed to look like. */
  .hero__pip {
    flex: 1 1 0;
    min-width: 0;
    height: 3px;
    border-radius: 2px;
    background: var(--line-control);
    animation: hero-pip step-end infinite;
    animation-duration: 42s;
  }

  /* The per-pip delays are GENERATED from the slide count -- see the inline
     style block at the foot of this file. They were six literals here until
     2026-08-19, which meant a seventh banner would light the wrong pip. */

  /* The checkbox itself is never seen; the label is the button. Not
     `display: none` — that would take it out of the tab order and off every
     assistive technology, which is the whole control gone. This keeps it
     focusable and reachable while the label carries the pixels. */
  .hero__toggle {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    margin: -1px;
    padding: 0;
    border: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .hero__pause {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    gap: 7px;
    padding: 5px 12px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 2px;
    background: var(--surface-raised);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--fs-label);
    font-weight: var(--fw-label);
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    /* grey-lt on card measures 8.90:1. `--color-grey` would be 4.14:1 here,
       which fails AA for text this size — 11px is nowhere near the 24px large
       bar. handoff.md §3. */
    color: var(--text-muted);
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .hero__pause::before {
    content: '';
    width: 7px;
    height: 8px;
    background: var(--accent-text);
    /* Two bars — the pause glyph — drawn rather than typed, so no font has to
       carry it and no text alternative is implied. */
    clip-path: polygon(0 0, 2px 0, 2px 8px, 0 8px, 5px 0, 7px 0, 7px 8px, 5px 8px);
  }

  .hero__pause:hover {
    border-color: var(--accent);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  /* The checkbox is visually hidden but still focusable, so the focus ring has
     to be drawn on the label the user can actually see. `:has()` again, because
     the label is not a sibling of the input any more — it is inside the control
     row, two levels down. */
  .hero__stage:has(.hero__toggle:focus-visible) .hero__pause {
    outline: 2px solid var(--accent-text);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  /* THE PAUSE ITSELF. `:has()` lets the checked state reach back up to the stage
     and stop both the track and the pips — they have to stop together or the lit
     pip drifts away from the visible slide.

     There used to be a third moving thing here — a pulsing red glow behind the
     stage, deliberately left running because it was ambient rather than
     informational. It was deleted with the white theme on 2026-08-20: a red
     radial bloom is a dark-surface device and on white it reads as a pink
     smudge. Nothing now moves in this hero except the track and the pips, and
     the pause control stops both. */
  /* THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO HOVER-PAUSE ON THIS CAROUSEL.

     One was added on 2026-08-29 — the design database's rule for auto-rotating
     media is to stop on hover, focus and reduced motion — and it was removed
     the same day, because `tests/e2e/hero-carousel.spec.ts` caught what it
     actually did: `.hero__controls` sits INSIDE `.hero__stage`, so the pointer
     that clicks Pause is hovering the stage, and pressing the control a second
     time left the band stopped. The toggle became a one-way door, which is the
     spec's own words for the thing that test exists to prevent.

     The band also has no links in it, so nothing here is a moving target the
     way the category ticker's names are — that is why `Ticker.astro` keeps its
     hover-pause and this does not. Generic carousel advice lost to a specific
     tested contract. Do not re-add it without moving the controls out of the
     hovered element first. */

  .hero__stage:has(.hero__toggle:checked) .hero__track,
  .hero__stage:has(.hero__toggle:checked) .hero__pip {
    animation-play-state: paused;
  }

  .hero__stage:has(.hero__toggle:checked) .hero__pause {
    color: var(--text);
    border-color: var(--accent);
  }

  .hero__stage:has(.hero__toggle:checked) .hero__pause::before {
    /* A right-pointing triangle: the control now says "Play". */
    clip-path: polygon(0 0, 7px 4px, 0 8px);
  }

  .hero__wrap {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    /* `center` until 2026-08-29, which shrink-wrapped every child to its own
       content width and then centred it. `stretch` is what lets the
       composition below own the full measure and place things within it. */
    align-items: stretch;
  }

  /* THE CREST, THE HEXAGONAL BADGE AND THE CLOSING UNDERLINE ARE GONE — all
     three removed 2026-08-29.

     They were the mockup's character and they were also three of the four
     competing horizontal rules a design review counted in a hero that is only
     about 600px tall: two red arms flanking the badge, and a notched red bar
     under the headline. Each was well made. Together they cut the composition
     into stripes and left nothing for the eye to travel along.

     What carries that job now is one VERTICAL red rule on the index, plus the
     masthead's slashes. If any of this comes back, something else has to go.

     ------------------------------------------------------------------------
     THE MASTHEAD — the technical system line that replaced the badge. */
  .hero__masthead {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0 10px;
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    /* 12px, up from 11. A third review said this line was one you had to
       search for, and offered two remedies: raise the size by about a pixel,
       OR raise the contrast by 15-20%. Not both.

       SIZE IS THE ONE TO TAKE HERE, and the reason is rule 4 rather than
       preference. --text-muted is 4.96:1 on --surface-alt, which clears the
       4.5:1 floor for normal-size text with very little room to spare in the
       other direction; there is no darker muted token, so "raise the contrast"
       would have meant inventing a colour and measuring it. A pixel is free
       and it is reversible. */
    font-size: var(--fs-meta);
    font-weight: var(--fw-label);
    letter-spacing: 0.2em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    animation: hero-rise 0.4s var(--ease-out) both;
  }

  .hero__masthead-item {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .hero__masthead-reg {
    font-size: 0.7em;
    vertical-align: super;
    letter-spacing: 0;
  }

  /* The only red at this size anywhere in the hero, and it is three glyphs.
     --accent-text, not brand red: 11px is normal-size text and --accent is
     4.30:1 on white. Rule 4. */
  .hero__masthead-sep {
    color: var(--accent-text);
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------
     THE COMPOSITION — headline left, counted totals right.

     `auto` on the second column, not a fraction: the index is as wide as its
     own numerals and never steals measure from the headline. Below 900px it
     drops under the type block and the whole thing becomes one left-aligned
     column, which is the phone layout and needs no separate rules. */
  .hero__composition {
    display: grid;
    /* `auto` for the numeral column: it is as wide as its own two digits and
       never steals measure from the headline. The gap collapses on a phone so
       the pair can share 320px the way every section head already does. */
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
    align-items: start;
    gap: 0 clamp(20px, 4vw, 56px);
    /* --hero-gap-close, not step: the masthead is an 11px line and 32px under
       it read as a hole once the stats strip left this block. */
    margin-top: var(--hero-gap-close);
  }

  /* The hero's numeral: same flow treatment as every section head, scaled by
     the same rule — a multiple of its own headline's clamp, so it is the
     largest on the page by construction and never by a hand-picked number.
     Its cap-top aligns with the headline's; its right edge is the wrap's,
     which the banner below now shares. */
  .hero__composition :global(.section-index) {
    justify-self: end;
    align-self: start;
    font-size: calc(clamp(36px, 5.4vw, 72px) * 2.4);
  }

  .hero__type {
    position: relative;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* THE OUTLINED PRODUCT COUNT. See the note in the markup for why this is the
     only element allowed to break the plane.

     `color: transparent` is load-bearing, not a trick: a browser with no
     `-webkit-text-stroke` support paints nothing, which is the failure this
     wants. Filling it and hoping would put a black slab over the headline.

     Right-anchored and pushed up behind the first line, so it never sits under
     "SOLUTIONS." — the red word is the one piece of type here whose contrast
     has the least headroom, and stacking texture behind it is how a measured
     ratio quietly stops being true. */

  /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------
     THE INDEX — the counted totals as the composition's counterweight.

     No horizontal rules between the rows. The temptation is a hairline per row
     and it is exactly what the review was complaining about; spacing separates
     them perfectly well and the single red vertical keeps them a group. */
  /* Pushed to the row's far end: the CTAs own the left edge, the totals own
     the right, and the red rule that leads them mirrors the numeral above on
     the same wrap edge. On a narrow screen `flex-wrap` drops the strip to its
     own line and `margin-left: auto` stops mattering. */
  .hero__index {
    display: grid;
    grid-auto-flow: column;
    justify-content: start;
    gap: 0 clamp(24px, 3vw, 44px);
    margin: 0 0 0 auto;
    padding-left: 22px;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
    animation: hero-rise 0.4s var(--ease-out) 0.14s both;
  }

  .hero__index-row {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2px;
  }

  .hero__index dt {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-micro);
    font-weight: var(--fw-label);
    letter-spacing: 0.2em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  /* Tabular figures, because these are data and a proportional 1 would leave
     the column visibly ragged against the 9s and 4s beside it. */
  .hero__index dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(30px, 3.2vw, 44px);
    font-weight: var(--fw-section);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  /* One sentence, one measure, and deliberately NOT the display face — the
     hero's job above this line is volume, and its job here is to be read.
     --text-muted is 4.96:1 on --surface-alt, clear of the 4.5:1 floor. */
  .hero__lede {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    /* 62ch -> 52ch -> 470px. A third review wanted roughly 430-480px: the line
       was travelling far enough horizontally to weaken the shape of the
       left-hand block the headline had just established.

       A pixel measure rather than `ch` on purpose. `ch` is the width of a "0"
       in the current face at the current size, and this rule's size is a
       clamp — so a `ch` cap silently changes measure at every viewport, which
       is the opposite of the fixed footprint being asked for. `min()` keeps it
       from overflowing a narrow column. */
    max-width: min(470px, 100%);
    margin: var(--hero-gap-close) 0 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: clamp(15px, 1.25vw, 17.5px);
    font-weight: var(--fw-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /*
   * FIRA SANS ITALIC, AND THE SKEW IS GONE WITH IT.
   *
   * The client supplied Fira Sans on 2026-08-29 for this headline. It is set
   * here and nowhere else — --font-display (Archivo) still serves every other
   * heading on the site, so this is one element, not a re-typesetting.
   *
   * The slant is now DRAWN rather than sheared. This rule used to carry
   * `transform: skewX(-9deg)`, which mechanically slanted upright Archivo, and
   * that transform brought two traps with it: it lost to any animation that
   * also set transform (hence a bespoke `hero-rise-lean` keyframe), and the
   * `font-style: oblique` attempt before it reported a slant Chrome never
   * synthesised. A real italic file removes the cause of both. The bespoke
   * keyframe is gone too — this is back on the shared `hero-rise`.
   *
   * WEIGHT 800 IS TWO THINGS AT ONCE. It exceeds --fw-display (500) on purpose,
   * which handoff.md §12 permits as "a ceiling per band, not a mandate" and §27
   * records. It is ALSO the weight the font file is pinned to: the axis is
   * pinned, not clipped, so any other value is silently clamped and renders
   * 800 anyway. tools/subset-hero-font.test.ts fails if this number moves
   * without the font being rebuilt.
   */
  /* THE HEADLINE IS A COMPOSITION NOW, NOT A CENTRED BLOCK.

     Two spans on one source line rather than a wrap point. It used to rely on
     `max-width: 940px` to push " SOLUTIONS." onto a second line — a measured
     cap, re-measured whenever the face changed, and still only a hint. The
     lines are explicit blocks now, which is what lets the second one be
     OFFSET and LARGER rather than merely underneath.

     The offset is the whole idea. Line one starts at the left margin; line two
     is pushed right and set at 1.18x, so the pair leans instead of stacking.
     Negative leading closes the gap between them until they nearly touch,
     which is what turns two lines of type into one shape. */
  .hero__title {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-hero);
    /* Ceiling 68px, down from 76px on 2026-08-29. The headline was never too
       big in isolation — it was too big relative to a 38px logo and an 11px
       nav, both of which went UP in the same pass. 68px is still comfortably
       "large text" by WCAG's definition, so --accent stays legal on it.

       The floor is untouched: 38px is what the phone was measured at. */
    /* The BASE size — line one. Line two multiplies it below.

       68 -> 72 at the ceiling, and the composition carries far more weight
       than the extra 4px suggests: line two lands at 85px. The review asked
       for roughly 62-70 and 82-94, which is where this sits. */
    font-size: clamp(36px, 5.4vw, 72px);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-style: italic;
    /* 0.96 -> 0.84 -> 0.74. The second step came from a third review: the two
       lines still read as "two formatted lines of an H1" rather than as one
       shape, and the fix it asked for was 8-12px of vertical separation taken
       out. At the 72px ceiling 0.84 gives a 60.5px line box and 0.74 gives
       53.3, so this is 7.2px at the top end and proportionally less below.

       It does not clip. The face is uppercase throughout — no descenders — and
       cap height is around 0.7em, so 0.74 still leaves the line box larger
       than the ink it carries. */
    line-height: 0.74;
    /* Fira's italic caps are wider than Archivo's at the same tracking, so this
       is looser than the -0.035em Archivo needed — see the measured table on
       max-width below, which is what set it. */
    letter-spacing: -0.045em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text);
    /* THE MEASURED WRAP POINT IS GONE, AND SO IS THE TABLE THAT SET IT.

       This rule used to carry `max-width: 940px` with a measured table of
       line-one and whole-string widths per face and tracking, because the cap
       was the only thing forcing " SOLUTIONS." onto its own line without a
       hard <br> that would be wrong on a phone.

       The lines are their own elements now, so the break is structural and
       cannot drift when the face or the tracking changes. What the cap used to
       protect is protected better. */
    /* `fit-content` is the staircase's whole mechanism. The h1 shrinks to its
       longest line — "HOME AND INDUSTRIAL" — and line two right-aligns inside
       that box, so the red word's last letter lands exactly under the black
       line's last letter at every viewport and every font size. The previous
       version pushed line two right by `clamp(0px, 7vw, 118px)`, a number that
       aligned with nothing and had to be hand-collapsed on phones. An edge
       shared with another element is a composition; a distance is a guess. */
    width: fit-content;
    animation: hero-rise 0.4s var(--ease-out) 0.06s both;
  }

  .hero__title-a,
  .hero__title-b {
    display: block;
  }

  .hero__title-b {
    text-align: right;
    /* Line two is bigger than line one — equal lines read as a stack, unequal
       lines that lean read as a composition. The offset itself is now the
       flush-right alignment above, not a margin. */
    font-size: 1.22em;
    /* Brand red is correct here — display type far over the 24px large-text
       bar where --accent's 4.30:1 is legal. */
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .hero__actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 16px;
    margin-top: var(--hero-gap-step);
    animation: hero-rise 0.4s var(--ease-out) 0.12s both;
  }

  /* THE TWO CTAs NOW READ AS ONE PAIR.

     SolidButton is uppercase display type in a square box; PillButton is
     sentence-case body type in a rounded one. Side by side that is three
     differences at once (case, family, shape), and it read as two controls
     from two different designs rather than a primary and a secondary. The
     client's mockup draws them as a matched pair.

     SCOPED TO THE HERO ON PURPOSE. PillButton is used on six other pages, and
     restyling the primitive would restyle all of them for a complaint about
     this one. `:global()` is required because the button is a child component
     and Astro's scoping does not reach into it. */
  /* THE HERO'S BUTTONS ARE BIGGER THAN THE SITE'S, and only here.

     After a 68px headline and a full-width banner, the primitives' default
     13.5px at 14px of padding read as a footnote — the two most valuable
     actions on the page were the smallest interactive things above the fold.
     The same button inside a card grid does not have that problem, so this is
     a hero override and NOT a change to SolidButton or PillButton.

     The 44px target floor is cleared by construction: 15px of type plus 18px
     of padding top and bottom is 51px. */
  .hero__actions :global(.solid) {
    padding: 18px 34px;
    font-size: var(--fs-body);
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  }

  .hero__actions :global(.pill) {
    border-radius: 0;
    padding: 17px 30px;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--fs-body-sm);
    font-weight: var(--fw-label);
    letter-spacing: 0.03em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    border-color: var(--text);
  }

  .hero__actions :global(.pill:hover) {
    border-color: var(--accent);
  }

  /*
   * `@keyframes hero-carousel` AND `@keyframes hero-pip` ARE GENERATED, in the
   * inline <style> at the foot of this file, because Astro's scoped styles
   * cannot interpolate a frontmatter value and the stops depend on the slide
   * count. At six banners they compute to exactly what used to be written here:
   * stops at 14.286% and 16.667%, a 42s cycle, and the last step landing on the
   * duplicate slide at -600%.
   *
   * There is deliberately no hold at 100%. A hold there would show the
   * duplicate for a beat and make the loop stutter once per cycle.
   */

  /*
   * `hero-pip`'s window is one slide's share of the cycle -- 16.667% at six
   * banners -- so it too is generated. Stepped rather than eased because a pip
   * that fades is ambiguous about which slide is actually showing.
   */

  /* THE FOUR USERS OF THIS RUN 0.4s, AND THEY USED TO RUN 0.7s. With
     `animation-fill-mode: both` the `from` state holds through the delay, so
     the longest chain — 0.7s plus a 0.2s delay — left the headline, the two
     CTAs and the counts fully transparent for the first ~0.9s of the page. A
     screenshot taken on load photographed an empty hero, and that is not a
     camera artefact: it is what a visitor on a slow device sees, and what
     Largest Contentful Paint records.

     0.4s with delays of 0.06 / 0.12 / 0.14 keeps the stagger legible and puts
     the worst case at ~0.54s. The delays stay proportional so the order still
     reads as one gesture rather than four separate ones.

     Do not answer this by deleting the opacity from the keyframes: both
     keyframes must keep a `transform`, or any `transform` declared on the same
     element is silently overwritten — docs/TRAPS.md has the case that cost. */
  @keyframes hero-rise {
    from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(16px); }
    to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }
  }

  /* Below this width the stage and the copy cannot coexist side by side.

     1180px, NOT the helmet's 1080px, and this is the one number the carousel
     genuinely had to move. The helmet's floor was measured on GLYPH pixels — the
     lit dome reaching the pill CTA's actual letterforms — and a transparent PNG
     whose mass stops well inside its own canvas can sit closer to the copy than
     an opaque rectangle can. Measured across widths with the card in place, the
     gap between the copy's rightmost ink and the card's left edge:

         1081px   -47px   the card sits ON the headline
         1100px   -28px   still overlapping
         1128px     0px   the crossover
         1160px   +32px   clear, but tight
         1240px  +112px   and constant above this, both sides being centred

     So 1080px would have shipped a card printed across the word "SOLUTIONS" on
     any window between 1081 and 1128 — an opaque poster over 76px display type,
     which is unreadable rather than merely tight. 1180px puts ~52px of clearance
     at the boundary. **A green axe run and a green verify both passed this**;
     it was found by measuring, which is the only thing that finds it.

     Stacking is the fix rather than shrinking the card, for the same reason the
     helmet chose stacking over a lower opacity: nothing is layered over anything
     here, so the card runs at full opacity and the copy sits on flat black.

     Stacking is the fix rather than a lower opacity. Nothing is layered over
     anything here, so the card runs at full opacity and the copy sits on flat
     black — which is what keeps the headline at 21:1 instead of measuring it
     against artwork.

     The stage also goes back into flow. Absolutely positioned it could not push
     the hero taller, so `overflow: hidden` would cut the card off.

     136px of top padding, not 96. The header is `position: absolute` and
     occupies y 0-128, so the hero's own padding-top is the only thing that
     clears it. 136px clears that box with 8px and the visible logo by 31px. */
  /* The strip tightens where the measure does — and gives up its right-edge
     anchor. `margin-left: auto` right-aligns it beside the CTAs on a desktop;
     on a phone the row has wrapped, the strip is alone on its line, and
     right-aligning a 350px group in a 320px column pushed "ESTABLISHED / 2015"
     past the edge where the hero's `overflow: hidden` clipped it without a
     scrollbar to confess. Left edge, tightened type, fits with room. */
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .hero__index {
      margin-left: 0;
      gap: 0 16px;
      padding-left: 14px;
    }

    .hero__index dt {
      font-size: var(--fs-micro);
      letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    }

    .hero__index dd {
      font-size: var(--fs-head-sm);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 1180px) {
    .hero {
      /* 86px of chrome plus 8px, matching the tighter 136/128 relationship
         this breakpoint carried before the utility bar was removed. */
      padding: 94px 0 64px;
    }
  }

  /* THE EMPTY SLOT OPENS OUT ON A PHONE, AND A FILLED ONE DOES NOT.
     `.hero__slot` only — `.hero__frame` keeps 4:1 at every width.

     The empty slot holds an icon and a line of label, and 84px is too short for
     either, so it takes the 3:2 the mobile mockup draws. A real banner has no
     such need and a great deal to lose: cropping 4:1 artwork into 3:2 discards
     about 60% of its width, which on the eye-protection banner means losing
     either the logo and headline or most of the products. Measured at 390px the
     band is 350 x 88 and the whole artwork is visible.

     DECIDED 2026-08-23, when the first real banner arrived and the comment that
     used to sit here said this would become a decision rather than a detail. It
     did. A short but complete band beats a tall crop of one sixth of the
     artwork. If a phone-shaped banner is ever wanted, it is a second upload and
     a second slot, not a crop of this one. */
  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .hero__slot {
      aspect-ratio: 3 / 2;
    }
  }

  /* PHONE WIDTHS ONLY — stacked and full-bleed. Wrapping in a row gave two
     rectangles of different widths with no shared edge. A column at the default
     `stretch` gives both the same width and a wider thumb target.

     560px, not 1080px: the two buttons need 392px to sit side by side, and
     stretching them across the copy's full 660px cap produced a 660px-wide
     button on a tablet. */
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .hero__actions {
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: stretch;
      gap: 12px;
    }
  }


  /* SHORT SCREENS BUY THE PRIMARY CTA BACK BY SPENDING CARD.
     Putting the CTAs under the stage costs above-the-fold visibility on a 667px
     screen. The helmet stage paid for that by shrinking to 58vw; a 4:5 portrait
     card is ~24% taller than that landscape stage at the same width AND the
     carousel added a 44px control row that WCAG 2.2.2 will not let us drop, so
     58vw no longer pays for it and the CTA lands 17px past the fold.

     38vw plus tightened gaps is what closes it. Measured on a 360x640 — the tighter of the two
     screens this has to hold on — the primary CTA's bottom edge lands at 612px
     against a 640px fold, with the same 20px and 24px margin trims the helmet
     version used. `tests/e2e/hero-mobile.spec.ts` asserts that edge on both
     screens, so the claim is checked rather than asserted.

     Keyed on HEIGHT, because this is a vertical-budget problem: a 390x844 phone
     has the room and keeps the larger card. It also catches a phone held in
     landscape, which has even less height and wants the same treatment. */
  /* THIS BLOCK OVERRIDES THE --hero-gap-* SCALE ON PURPOSE. The values below
     are not steps on that scale; they are trims measured against a 640px fold.
     Keep them literal — naming them would imply they move with the scale, and
     they must not. */
  @media (max-width: 1180px) and (max-height: 700px) {
    /* THE HEADER RESTYLE SPENT THIS BUDGET AND NOBODY NOTICED, because the
       suite that measures it could not run — see handoff.md §29. The crest, the
       three-line oblique headline and the closing rule arrived on 2026-08-23
       and pushed the primary CTA's bottom edge to 646px against a 640px fold:
       the one control the site converts on, 6px under the crease, on the
       tighter of the two screens this block exists for. CI caught it on
       2026-08-27, four commits later.

       THE TRIM COMES FROM RHYTHM, NOT FROM ANY OF THE THREE NEW ELEMENTS. The
       crest, the headline's slant and the rule are the mockup's character and
       the client asked for them; shrinking one to buy pixels would be undoing
       the design to satisfy a test. What these two margins carry is 48px of
       space between them at every height, which is generous on a desktop and
       extravagant on a screen with 640px in total. They are the only numbers
       here that are taste rather than structure.

       Not the hero's own 94px top padding: the header's bottom edge is at
       86px on this screen, so that padding is clearance, and spending it
       would put the eyebrow under the header. Not the stage either — its 3:2
       ratio below 720px is pinned by a test and was a deliberate call.

       Measured after the trim on a 360x640: the primary CTA's bottom edge lands
       at 620px, restoring the 20px of clearance the block was written with.
       `tests/e2e/hero-mobile.spec.ts` asserts that edge on both screens. */
    /* The composition carries the top gap now; the headline sits inside it and
       no longer has a margin of its own to trim. */
    .hero__composition {
      margin-top: 12px;
    }

    /* The index sits under the type on one column here, so it needs its own
       gap rather than the grid's. */
    .hero__index {
      gap: 12px;
      margin-top: 20px;
    }

    /* The band is full-width now, so there is no card width left to shrink —
       only vertical gaps. The stage keeps its ratio and the trims come out of
       the space around it. */
    .hero__stage {
      margin-top: 16px;
    }

    /* The control row is a WCAG 2.2.2 requirement whenever a carousel is
       actually running, so it cannot be dropped to buy space; its gap can be
       tightened instead. */
    /* The bar carries the gap now, not the controls. On the narrowest screens
       the label and the rail stack rather than squeezing to nothing. */
    .hero__bar {
      margin-bottom: 8px;
    }

    .hero__actions {
      margin-top: 16px;
    }

    /* THE 2026-08-29 PASS SPENT THIS BUDGET AND PAID IT BACK HERE.

       Removing the utility bar returned 42px of top padding (136 -> 94). The
       supporting sentence then took roughly 55 of it and the taller CTAs
       another 8, so without these two rules the primary CTA's bottom edge
       lands past the 640px crease again — the exact regression the block above
       records from 2026-08-23.

       The sentence is NOT dropped on a phone. It is the change that answers
       "what does this company sell?", and a phone is where that question gets
       asked most. It is set tighter instead, and the CTAs hand back the
       padding they gained, which is a size change rather than a content one.

       Measured, not estimated: see the note above. Re-measure both screens if
       any of these move. */
    .hero__lede {
      margin-top: 8px;
      font-size: var(--fs-body-sm);
      line-height: 1.45;
    }

    .hero__actions :global(.solid),
    .hero__actions :global(.pill) {
      padding-block: 13px;
    }
  }

  /* Nothing moves, and the entrance animations resolve to their end state rather
     than never running — `both` on a cancelled animation would leave the copy at
     opacity 0.

     `src/styles/global.css` also collapses every animation site-wide here with
     `!important`, but it only forces animation-duration and iteration-count. The
     `animation: none` shorthand below sets animation-name, which that rule does
     not touch, so these declarations still take effect.

     THE CAROUSEL STOPS ON SLIDE ONE, and the pause control is hidden with it.
     A control that pauses something already stopped is a lie about what it does,
     and it was the only thing in the hero that could take focus and do nothing.
     Ticker.astro hides its own control here for the same reason. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    /* `.hero__glow` was the third selector in this list until 2026-08-20.
       Deleting it took the closing brace with it and left `.hero__track` and
       `.hero__pip` dangling onto the rule below — so the carousel kept
       animating for a visitor who had asked it not to, while the page still
       looked correct. tests/e2e/motion.spec.ts caught it; nothing else would
       have. */
    .hero__track,
    .hero__pip {
      animation: none;
    }

    .hero__pip:first-child {
      background: var(--accent);
      width: 26px;
    }
    .hero__pause,
    .hero__toggle {
      display: none;
    }
    /* THIS LIST HAS TO TRACK EVERY ANIMATED ELEMENT IN THE HERO, and nothing
       fails when it does not — the element simply keeps animating for a
       visitor who asked their device for less motion, and the page still looks
       correct to everyone else. `tests/e2e/motion.spec.ts` is the only thing
       that catches it.

       `.hero__crest` and `.hero__underline` left on 2026-08-29 with the
       elements themselves; `.hero__masthead` and `.hero__index` arrived in the
       same pass and carry `hero-rise` now. */
    .hero__masthead,
    .hero__index,
    .hero__title,
    .hero__actions {
      animation: none;
      opacity: 1;
      transform: none;
    }

    .hero__stage {
      transition: none;
    }
  }
</style>

